{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/stories/03-turn-construction-recovery-cascade.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Story 3: Turn Construction and Recovery Cascade\n     2\t\n     3\t### Summary\n     4\t<!-- Jira: Summary field -->\n     5\t\n     6\tResolve turn components through the recovery cascade, compose complete turns, log fallbacks, and write resolved outputs back safely.\n     7\t\n     8\t### Description\n     9\t<!-- Jira: Description field -->\n    10\t\n    11\t**User Profile:** the harness/operator running long-horizon agentic work whose threads must keep serving coherent context even when background derivation lags, fails, or hits damaged sources.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Objective:** instantiate the floor-and-recovery contract at turn construction so turns never block or omit spans because a derivation is pending or failed.\n    14\t\n    15\t**Scope In:**\n    16\t- Ready derivations used directly.\n    17\t- Pending and failed derivations handled identically through recovery.\n    18\t- Deterministic floor then original-source fallback, except tool results never fall below truncation.\n    19\t- Non-derived components placed verbatim.\n    20\t- Fallback logging.\n    21\t- Recovery write-back to `ready` except when live work remains claimed or pending.\n    22\t- Turn-construction recovery may call provider outside DB transaction.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Scope Out:**\n    25\t- Compact-time recovery, owned by Story 4.\n    26\t- Logging storage implementation, owned by Story 0.\n    27\t\n    28\t**Dependencies:** Stories 1 and 2.\n    29\t\n    30\t### Acceptance Criteria\n    31\t<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\n    32\t\n    33\t**AC-3.1:** When a component's derivation is `ready`, turn construction uses it directly.\n    34\t\n    35\t- **TC-3.1a:** Ready component used\n    36\t  - Given: a turn whose smoothed prompt is `ready`\n    37\t  - When: the turn is constructed\n    38\t  - Then: the smoothed prompt is used as-is\n    39\t\n    40\t**AC-3.2:** When a component's derivation is not ready (`pending` or `failed`, treated identically), construction attempts recovery and then falls through deterministic floor → original, always landing a usable component.\n    41\t\n    42\t- **TC-3.2a:** Pending recovers to floor\n    43\t  - Given: a turn whose smoothed prompt is `pending` and re-derivation does not complete\n    44\t  - When: the turn is constructed\n    45\t  - Then: the deterministic-cleaned prompt is used and the turn still constructs\n    46\t- **TC-3.2b:** Failed handled like pending\n    47\t  - Given: a turn whose smoothed prompt is `failed`\n    48\t  - When: the turn is constructed\n    49\t  - Then: the same cascade runs (no separate failed handling) and a usable component results\n    50\t- **TC-3.2c:** Floor unavailable → original\n    51\t  - Given: a component whose deterministic floor cannot be produced\n    52\t  - When: the turn is constructed\n    53\t  - Then: the original source content is used\n    54\t\n    55\t**AC-3.3:** A tool-result component never falls below its truncation floor during construction; it is never inserted raw/full as a fallback.\n    56\t\n    57\t- **TC-3.3a:** Tool result floored to truncation\n    58\t  - Given: a turn whose `tool_result_summary` is not ready\n    59\t  - When: the turn is constructed\n    60\t  - Then: the deterministic truncation is used, not the full raw result\n    61\t\n    62\t**AC-3.4:** Non-derived components (assistant text, assistant thinking, runtime-change blocks) are placed verbatim in the constructed turn.\n    63\t\n    64\t- **TC-3.4a:** Verbatim placement\n    65\t  - Given: a turn with assistant text, thinking, and a runtime-change block\n    66\t  - When: the turn is constructed\n    67\t  - Then: those components appear unchanged and in order\n    68\t\n    69\t**AC-3.5:** Turn construction never blocks on a derivation and never omits a component span; a not-ready derivation degrades, it does not stall or hole the turn.\n    70\t\n    71\t- **TC-3.5a:** No block, no hole\n    72\t  - Given: a turn with multiple not-ready derivations\n    73\t  - When: the turn is constructed\n    74\t  - Then: construction completes and every component is present as some rendering\n    75\t\n    76\t**AC-3.6:** Every component that fell back during construction is logged with enough detail to act on it: derivation type, subject id, why it floored (not-ready vs failed-floor), and which floor was used.\n    77\t\n    78\t- **TC-3.6a:** Fallback logged with detail\n    79\t  - Given: a turn construction where a smoothed prompt fell to its deterministic floor\n    80\t  - When: construction completes\n    81\t  - Then: a log entry records the derivation type, subject id, reason, and floor used\n    82\t- **TC-3.6b:** No fallback → no fallback log\n    83\t  - Given: a turn where all derivations are ready\n    84\t  - When: construction completes\n    85\t  - Then: no fallback log entries are written for it\n    86\t\n    87\t**AC-3.7:** Turn construction performs no provider work inside a DB write transaction.\n    88\t\n    89\t- **TC-3.7a:** Provider work outside transaction\n    90\t  - Given: turn construction attempts re-derivation of a not-ready component\n    91\t  - When: it runs\n    92\t  - Then: any provider call happens outside the write transaction that persists the turn\n    93\t\n    94\t**AC-3.8:** When turn-construction recovery resolves a not-ready component — by re-derivation, deterministic floor, or original-source floor — the resolved content is written back to the component's derivation record as `ready`, **except when a work item is still claimed or pending for that derivation**, in which case the record is left untouched (the floor is used in the rendering, but the in-flight worker is allowed to produce the real result). Persisted recovery outcomes land plain `ready`; there is no \"degraded\" state and no floor-used marker (no upgrade process consumes such a marker). The fallback *event* is logged; the canonical subject and any `ready` output carry no marker. A tool result's floor is deterministic truncation, never the raw full result.\n    95\t\n    96\t- **TC-3.8a:** Re-derivation lands ready\n    97\t  - Given: a `failed` component that recovery re-derives successfully\n    98\t  - When: construction completes\n    99\t  - Then: the derivation record is `ready` with the re-derived content\n   100\t- **TC-3.8b:** Floor recovery also lands ready\n   101\t  - Given: a `pending` component that recovery cannot re-derive, resolved at its deterministic floor\n   102\t  - When: construction completes\n   103\t  - Then: the derivation record is `ready` with the floored content, carries no degraded marker, and the fallback event is logged\n   104\t- **TC-3.8c:** Tool-result floor is truncation, never raw\n   105\t  - Given: a tool-result component whose summary is not ready and cannot be re-derived\n   106\t  - When: construction resolves it\n   107\t  - Then: the floor written back is deterministic truncation, never the raw full result\n   108\t- **TC-3.8d:** Write-back defers to live work\n   109\t  - Given: a not-ready component that still has a claimed or pending work item\n   110\t  - When: construction resolves it at the floor\n   111\t  - Then: the floor is used in the rendering but the derivation record is left untouched (not overwritten), so the in-flight worker still produces the real `ready` result\n   112\t\n   113\t### Technical Design\n   114\t<!-- Jira: Technical Notes or sub-section of Description -->\n   115\t\n   116\t#### Architecture Context\n   117\t\n   118\tStory 3 turns the existing compose fallback behavior into durable recovery. `composeRenderingInput` resolves ready-or-floor parts and records gaps; `derive` logs each fallback and calls `recoverDerivation(...)` for recovered content. Persisted recovery lands plain `ready`; the fallback event is log-only.\n   119\t\n   120\tTurn-construction recovery may call the provider, but the provider call sits outside any DB write transaction. `recoverDerivation(...)` is a dedicated version-checked short transaction and skips persistence when a live work item is pending or claimed, leaving the worker to produce the real result.\n   121\t\n   122\t#### Build Strategy\n   123\t\n   124\tStrategy: full-staged-risk\n   125\t\n   126\tReason:\n   127\t- This story combines consumption-time recovery, logging, source/derived state separation, write-back, and provider transaction boundaries.\n   128\t- The race between floor write-back and live worker completion needs explicit red/green coverage.\n   129\t\n   130\tRisk Reminders:\n   131\t- Transition-state atomicity: recovered content writes `ready` only when source version matches and no live work exists.\n   132\t- Concurrency/lost update: floor write must not clobber in-flight real derivation.\n   133\t- Provider outside transaction: prove with a competing write during provider call.\n   134\t\n   135\t#### Implementation Targets\n   136\t\n   137\t| Area | Files / Modules |\n   138\t|------|-----------------|\n   139\t| Compose fallback receipts | `packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/compose.ts` |\n   140\t| Recovery write-back | `packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/derive.ts`, `packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/store.ts` |\n   141\t| Recovery helper | `packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/recovery.ts` (inferred target for `recoverDerivation`) |\n   142\t| Logging calls | `packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/logging/index.ts` |\n   143\t| Provider doubles | `packages/lhc/test/fixtures/provider-double.ts` |\n   144\t| Story tests | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` (planned) |\n   145\t\n   146\t#### Design References\n   147\t\n   148\t- [tech-design.md §DD-4](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/tech-design.md:113), lines 113-125\n   149\t- [tech-design.md §DD-7](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/tech-design.md:140), lines 140-144\n   150\t- [tech-design.md §Recovery write-back](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/tech-design.md:214), lines 214-224\n   151\t- [tech-design.md §Cascade write-back mechanics](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/tech-design.md:266), lines 266-281\n   152\t- [tech-design.md §Architecture-Risk Tests](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/tech-design.md:299), lines 299-309\n   153\t- [test-plan.md §Flow 3](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/test-plan.md:64), lines 64-81\n   154\t- [test-plan.md §Architecture-Risk Tests](/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/docs/specs/01-lhc-sdk/06-derivation-recovery/test-plan.md:121), lines 121-135\n   155\t\n   156\t#### Test Mapping\n   157\t\n   158\t| TC | Test File / Check | Test Description |\n   159\t|----|-------------------|------------------|\n   160\t| TC-3.1a | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Ready smoothed prompt is used as-is in turn construction. |\n   161\t| TC-3.2a | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Pending smoothed prompt recovers to deterministic floor and turn constructs. |\n   162\t| TC-3.2b | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Failed smoothed prompt uses same cascade path as pending. |\n   163\t| TC-3.2c | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Component with unavailable floor falls back to original source. |\n   164\t| TC-3.3a | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Not-ready tool-result summary uses truncation, never raw full result. |\n   165\t| TC-3.4a | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Assistant text, thinking, and runtime-change blocks render verbatim in order. |\n   166\t| TC-3.5a | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Multiple not-ready derivations still produce a complete turn with every span present. |\n   167\t| TC-3.6a | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Fallback log includes derivation type, subject id, reason, and floor used. |\n   168\t| TC-3.6b | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | All-ready turn construction writes no fallback logs. |\n   169\t| TC-3.7a | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | `probingProvider` competing write acquires lock while provider call is pending. |\n   170\t| TC-3.8a | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Failed component re-derived successfully persists `ready` through `recoverDerivation`. |\n   171\t| TC-3.8b | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Pending component resolved to floor persists `ready` with no degraded marker and logs fallback. |\n   172\t| TC-3.8c | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Tool-result recovery writes truncation floor, never raw full result. |\n   173\t| TC-3.8d | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Live claimed/pending work causes `recoverDerivation` to return `persisted:false`; worker later wins. |\n   174\t\n   175\t#### Architecture-Risk Tests\n   176\t\n   177\t| Risk | Test File / Check | Test Description | Why AC/TC Mapping Alone Would Miss It |\n   178\t|------|-------------------|------------------|---------------------------------------|\n   179\t| No transaction across provider | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Competing SQLite write succeeds while provider call is pending. | A delayed provider test does not prove the DB lock was released. |\n   180\t| Recovery write-back defers to live work | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Claimed work item exists, floor renders but does not persist, later worker completion persists real output. | Simple write-back tests miss the race with in-flight workers. |\n   181\t| Stale recovery discarded | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Version-bump variant proves `recoverDerivation` refuses stale source version. | AC happy paths can pass while stale writes remain possible. |\n   182\t| Source/derived separation | `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` | Canonical subject has no fallback marker; recovered ready output has no floor marker. | Log presence alone does not prove subject cleanliness. |\n   183\t\n   184\t#### Technical Notes\n   185\t\n   186\t- `recoverDerivation(...)` is not `complete()` and requires no `ClaimedWorkItem`.\n   187\t- It writes in one short `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, version-checks source, and checks for live pending/claimed work.\n   188\t- Tool-result floor remains deterministic truncation; raw full tool output is never a fallback rendering.\n   189\t- Use Story 0 logging surface; do not create a turn-local diagnostics table.\n   190\t\n   191\t#### Anti-Shim Requirements\n   192\t\n   193\t- Do not mark derivations `ready` before checking live pending/claimed work.\n   194\t- Do not hold a transaction open across provider calls.\n   195\t- Do not encode floor provenance on the canonical subject or ready derivation content.\n   196\t- Do not make failed and pending derivations take separate recovery logic.\n   197\t\n   198\t#### Production Path Proof\n   199\t\n   200\t- Entrypoint: turn close invokes turn derivation and composition through `domains/turns`.\n   201\t- Registration/default path: existing work-queue turn handlers call compose/derive; recovery runs in the same production handler path, not a test-only helper.\n   202\t- Evidence: `packages/lhc/test/turn-cascade.test.ts` exercises the production compose/derive path with real storage and provider doubles.\n   203\t\n   204\t#### Verification\n   205\t\n   206\t- Targeted: `cd packages/lhc && pnpm run test -- test/turn-cascade.test.ts`\n   207\t- Story gate: `cd packages/lhc && pnpm run verify`\n   208\t- Epic gate: `cd packages/lhc && pnpm run verify-all`\n   209\t\n   210\t#### Spec Deviations\n   211\t\n   212\tNone.\n   213\t\n   214\t### Definition of Done\n   215\t<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\n   216\t\n   217\t- AC-3.1 through AC-3.8 pass with their listed TCs.\n   218\t- Pending and failed derivations share one recovery path.\n   219\t- Turn-construction recovery provider calls, when used, occur outside DB write transactions.\n   220\t- Fallback logs include derivation type, subject id, reason, and floor used.\n   221\t- Tool-result fallback writes truncation, never raw full result.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
